I would not use a single [442.033, 370.370] cell size. The fire grid is stored on lat/lon coordinates, not a Cartesian grid with constant meter spacing. The values in the first row of the terrain file are only approximate reference dimensions, not the correct area for every grid cell.

Use the .geom file instead: it has NX NY NZ, followed by the longitudes, latitudes, and cos(latitudes). Compute cell area row-by-row using the longitude spacing, latitude spacing, and cos(latitude). For this domain the center-cell size is closer to about 370 m × 370 m, and the east-west dimension varies slightly with latitude.



For each latitude row j, keep dy constant and scale dx by cos(latitude_j).

Use:

dy=RΔϕdy=RΔϕdxj=Rcos⁡(ϕj)Δλdxj​=Rcos(ϕj​)Δλareaj=dxj dyareaj​=dxj​dy

where:

R = 6371000 m
phi_j is the latitude of row j in radians
Δphi is latitude spacing in radians
Δlambda is longitude spacing in radians

In code:

import numpy as np

R = 6371000.0

lons = np.array(lons_from_geom)   # length NX
lats = np.array(lats_from_geom)   # length NY

dlon = np.deg2rad(abs(lons[1] - lons[0]))
dlat = np.deg2rad(abs(lats[1] - lats[0]))

dy = R * dlat
dx_by_row = R * np.cos(np.deg2rad(lats)) * dlon
area_by_row = dx_by_row * dy

# full 2D area array, NY x NX
area = np.repeat(area_by_row[:, None], len(lons), axis=1)
So every cell in the same latitude row gets the same area; area changes only north–south.









The first line of the .terrain file is a header:

nx ny nz dx dy

where:

nx = number of grid points in the west–east direction.
ny = number of grid points in the south–north direction.
nz = number of vertical levels in the associated 3D ASC files.
dx = approximate west–east grid spacing (meters) at the center of the domain. This value has already been divided by cos(center latitude) because the model uses latitude/longitude coordinates. The true east–west grid spacing varies slightly with latitude.
dy = approximate north–south grid spacing (meters) at the center of the domain. This is essentially constant across the domain.
The remainder of the .terrain file contains the terrain elevation (meters above mean sea level) for each model grid cell.

The .geom file contains metadata describing the model grid. Its first line has the same dimensions (nx ny nz).

It is followed by:

nx longitude values giving the longitude of each west–east grid column.
ny latitude values giving the latitude of each south–north grid row.
ny values of cos(latitude) corresponding to each grid row, from the southernmost to the northernmost row.
These latitude and cosine values allow the actual east–west grid spacing for any row to be computed. If dx_header is the value in the header, then for a row with latitude φ:

dx(ϕ)=dx_header​ × cos(ϕ)

Thus, each row has a slightly different physical east–west cell width, while the north–south spacing remains essentially constant.
